Music owns the buffer it streams from (crash in GC, e.g. on PSDK soft reset).

open_from_memory only kept the Ruby String in @memory while sf::Music
streams from its buffer on its own thread. When a Music and its String
became garbage in the same GC run, Ruby could free the String first and
the Music then read freed memory. Copy the bytes into the native object.

Stopping a Music seeks its file, reading the buffer, and both
Music::openFromMemory and ~Music() stop first:
- opening another buffer keeps the current one until the new file is open
  (crash when a map changes its BGM);
- the buffer is a base declared before sf::Music, so it is destroyed after.

Applied on top of the pinned NuriYuri/sfmlaudio revision; drop it once
upstream carries an equivalent fix.

diff --git a/ext/SFMLAudio/sf_Music.cpp b/ext/SFMLAudio/sf_Music.cpp
index fdb1ac0..d612c1d 100644
--- a/ext/SFMLAudio/sf_Music.cpp
+++ b/ext/SFMLAudio/sf_Music.cpp
@@ -1,8 +1,27 @@
 #include "rb_common.h"
+#include <vector>
 
 
 VALUE rb_cMusic = Qnil;
 
+// sf::Music streams straight from the buffer given to openFromMemory, on its
+// own thread. Keeping only the Ruby String alive (@memory) is not enough:
+// when a Music and its String become garbage in the same GC run, Ruby may
+// free the String first, and the Music (still streaming, or closing its
+// decoder) then reads freed memory. Own a copy instead.
+// ~Music() stops the stream, and stopping seeks the file, reading the
+// buffer: the buffer is a base declared before sf::Music, destroyed after it.
+struct RbMusicMemory {
+  std::vector<char> memory;
+};
+
+class RbMusic : private RbMusicMemory, public sf::Music {
+public:
+  using RbMusicMemory::memory;
+};
+
+// The Ruby object wraps the sf::Music (not the RbMusic, whose sf::Music is
+// not at its start): every SoundStream / SoundSource method reads it so.
 void rb_Music_Free(void* data)
 {
   sf::Music* pdata = reinterpret_cast<sf::Music*>(data);
@@ -11,7 +30,7 @@ void rb_Music_Free(void* data)
 
 VALUE rb_Music_Alloc(VALUE klass)
 {
-  sf::Music* data = new sf::Music();
+  sf::Music* data = new RbMusic();
   return Data_Wrap_Struct(klass, NULL, rb_Music_Free, data);
 }
 
@@ -24,8 +43,14 @@ VALUE rb_MopenFromFile(VALUE self, VALUE filename) {
 VALUE rb_MopenFromMemory(VALUE self, VALUE string) {
   Check_Type(string, T_STRING);
   RB_GET_DATA(sf::Music, Qfalse);
+  RbMusic* music = static_cast<RbMusic*>(data);
   rb_iv_set(self, "@memory", string);
-  return data->openFromMemory(StringValuePtr(string), RSTRING_LEN(string)) ? Qtrue : Qfalse;
+  // openFromMemory first stops the current file, and stopping seeks it back
+  // to the start: it still reads the current buffer. Keep that buffer until
+  // the new file is open (moving a vector keeps its buffer in place).
+  std::vector<char> previous(std::move(music->memory));
+  music->memory.assign(RSTRING_PTR(string), RSTRING_PTR(string) + RSTRING_LEN(string));
+  return music->openFromMemory(music->memory.data(), music->memory.size()) ? Qtrue : Qfalse;
 }
 
